CAT = "ffiSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
Q = [
("The principle that each firefighter reports to only one supervisor is called:",
 "Unity of command", ["Span of control", "Division of labor", "Chain of custody"],
 "Unity of command means every member answers to one supervisor at a time. Span of control is how many people one supervisor can manage effectively."),
("Under the Incident Command System, the recommended span of control for one supervisor is:",
 "Three to seven people", ["One to two people", "Eight to twelve people", "Ten to fifteen people"],
 "ICS keeps span of control between three and seven, with five as the usual optimum. Beyond that, the incident commander adds divisions, groups or branches."),
("Which ICS position is part of the command staff rather than the general staff?",
 "Safety officer", ["Operations section chief", "Planning section chief", "Logistics section chief"],
 "The command staff is the safety officer, the public information officer and the liaison officer. Operations, planning, logistics and finance/administration make up the general staff."),
("Who is responsible for every incident until command is formally transferred?",
 "The first-arriving officer", ["The highest-ranking chief on duty", "The dispatch supervisor", "The last unit to arrive"],
 "The first-arriving officer or member establishes command and keeps it until a higher-ranking officer formally takes it over. Dispatch supports the incident but does not command it."),
("An OSHA respiratory rule requires at least two firefighters outside when two enter an IDLH atmosphere. This is known as:",
 "Two-in, two-out", ["The buddy system", "Rapid intervention only", "Command and control"],
 "OSHA's respiratory protection standard requires at least two members outside, ready to rescue, whenever two enter an atmosphere that is immediately dangerous to life or health. One of the two outside may hold another role, such as incident commander, if it does not stop them from acting."),
("A team staged at a fire with the sole job of rescuing lost or trapped firefighters is a:",
 "Rapid intervention crew", ["Search and rescue group", "Ventilation group", "Staging area crew"],
 "A rapid intervention crew (RIC or RIT) stands by with tools and air to rescue firefighters. A search group looks for civilians, not members."),
("A roll call of all crews ordered by the incident commander is a:",
 "Personnel accountability report", ["Size-up report", "Situation status report", "Mutual aid request"],
 "A personnel accountability report (PAR) confirms every assigned member is accounted for. It is often ordered after a mayday, a change from offensive to defensive, or at set time intervals."),
("A firefighter who is lost and low on air should first:",
 "Call a mayday", ["Remove the SCBA facepiece", "Search for the exit alone", "Wait for a PAR"],
 "Call the mayday early, then give location, unit, name, assignment and air, and resources needed (LUNAR). Searching alone burns air while no one knows you are in trouble."),
("In the LUNAR mayday report, the letter A stands for:",
 "Assignment and air", ["Address and apparatus", "Alarm and agency", "Area and access"],
 "LUNAR is location, unit, name, assignment and air, and resources needed. Air remaining tells the rescuers how fast they must reach you."),
("The leading cause of on-duty firefighter deaths in the United States is:",
 "Sudden cardiac events", ["Structural collapse", "Burns in flashover", "Smoke inhalation"],
 "Heart attacks and other stress and overexertion events cause the largest share of on-duty deaths year after year, which is why fitness, medical exams and rehab matter. Vehicle crashes are usually second."),
("When riding on fire apparatus, a firefighter must:",
 "Be seated and belted", ["Stand on the tailboard", "Ride on the side step", "Unbuckle to don SCBA"],
 "Every member rides seated with the seat belt fastened whenever the apparatus moves. Riding on tailboards or steps is prohibited, and SCBA is donned only if it can be done while belted."),
("While an apparatus is backing up, a spotter should stand:",
 "At the left rear, visible", ["Directly behind the bumper", "In the cab beside the driver", "At the front of the truck"],
 "The spotter stands at the left rear corner where the driver can see them in the mirror. A spotter directly behind the vehicle can be struck if the driver loses sight of them."),
("Which step after a fire most reduces a firefighter's cancer risk?",
 "Gross decon of gear on scene", ["Leaving gear on in quarters", "Storing gear in the cab", "Washing gear once a year"],
 "Rinsing and brushing off gear on scene, wiping exposed skin, changing hoods and showering soon after reduce exposure to carcinogens. Gear should never be worn in living quarters or carried in the cab."),
("A firefighter in traffic on a highway, not engaged in fire suppression, should wear:",
 "A high-visibility vest", ["Only the turnout coat", "A white helmet", "A reflective hood"],
 "High-visibility safety vests are worn when working in or near moving traffic. Turnout coats are exempt only when the member faces fire or hazardous materials exposure."),
("When taking a report of a fire, the telecommunicator's first priority is the:",
 "Location of the emergency", ["Caller's full name", "What started the fire", "Whether anyone is inside"],
 "Without the address or location, no unit can be sent. The callback number and nature of the emergency come next."),
("Before speaking after pressing the radio transmit button, a firefighter should:",
 "Pause about one second", ["Speak immediately", "Shout to be heard", "Wait for a ten-second tone"],
 "A short pause lets the radio system open the channel so the first words are not clipped. Speaking in a normal voice, close to the microphone, is clearer than shouting."),
("A radio message that all units must stop routine traffic to hear is called:",
 "Emergency traffic", ["Clear text", "Routine traffic", "A ten-code"],
 "Emergency traffic clears the channel for an urgent message, such as an order to evacuate. Clear text is plain language, which is preferred for all messages."),
("Written guidelines that tell members how a department handles routine tasks are:",
 "Standard operating procedures", ["Mutual aid agreements", "Incident action plans", "Preincident planning surveys"],
 "SOPs or SOGs set the department's expected actions. An incident action plan applies to one incident; mutual aid agreements cover help between departments."),
("An agreement where a neighboring department is dispatched automatically on the first alarm is:",
 "Automatic aid", ["Mutual aid", "Outside aid", "Contract dispatch"],
 "Automatic aid sends units without a request, often to areas near a boundary. Mutual aid is sent only when the host department asks for it."),
("In the DOT Emergency Response Guidebook, the orange-bordered pages contain:",
 "Response guides", ["Index by ID number", "Index by name", "Protective distances"],
 "Orange pages hold the numbered guides with hazards and actions. Yellow pages index by ID number, blue pages by material name, and green pages give isolation and protective action distances."),
("At a suspected hazardous materials release, responders should approach from:",
 "Upwind and uphill", ["Downwind and uphill", "Upwind and downhill", "Downwind and downhill"],
 "Upwind keeps vapors blowing away from responders, and uphill keeps liquids and heavy gases flowing away from them. Upstream is the equivalent for waterways."),
("The first action an awareness-level responder takes at a hazmat incident is to:",
 "Isolate and deny entry", ["Plug the leaking container", "Neutralize the product", "Enter to read the label"],
 "Awareness-level responders recognize the hazard, isolate the area, deny entry and call for trained help. Plugging or neutralizing is an operations or technician task."),
("A diamond-shaped sign on a tank truck showing a hazard class is a:",
 "Placard", ["Label", "Marking", "Shipping paper"],
 "Placards are 10.75-inch diamonds on vehicles and bulk containers. Labels are the smaller diamonds on individual packages, and shipping papers ride in the cab."),
("A firefighter gets to rehab after two SCBA cylinders. The main purposes of rehab are rest, medical monitoring and:",
 "Rehydration and cooling", ["Refilling SCBA cylinders", "Writing the report", "Cleaning the hose"],
 "Rehab lets members rest, cool down, drink fluids and be checked for heat stress and cardiac warning signs before going back to work. Equipment tasks belong elsewhere."),
("An atmosphere with less than what oxygen level is considered oxygen deficient?",
 "19.5 percent", ["21 percent", "23.5 percent", "16 percent"],
 "Air is about 21 percent oxygen; below 19.5 percent it is oxygen deficient and SCBA is required. Above 23.5 percent is oxygen enriched, which raises the fire hazard."),
("The incident management system officer who can stop an unsafe act immediately is the:",
 "Incident safety officer", ["Liaison officer", "Public information officer", "Staging officer"],
 "The incident safety officer has the authority to halt or prevent unsafe acts on the spot. The liaison officer coordinates with other agencies, and the information officer deals with the media."),
("When a building is no longer safe to enter, the fire is fought from outside. This is called a:",
 "Defensive operation", ["Offensive operation", "Transitional search", "Marginal attack"],
 "Defensive operations protect exposures and attack from outside. Offensive operations put crews inside to attack the fire directly."),
("The most important reason to keep a firefighter accountability system at every incident is to:",
 "Know who is where", ["Track overtime hours", "Count hose used", "Record radio traffic"],
 "Accountability tags, boards or electronic systems tell command which members are assigned where, so a missing member is noticed quickly. Payroll and equipment counts are not its purpose."),
]
